{{<shell}}{{$content}}
<h1>Engagement</h1>
<p class="hint">Every rule this server follows for getting somebody here and getting them back,
  in one place. Almost none of it is a switch, and that is deliberate: this is the part of a
  community that goes wrong by being tuned.</p>

<div class="cards">
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{online}}</div><div class="l">here now</div></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{sent}}</div><div class="l">invited</div></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{joined}}</div><div class="l">joined</div></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{conversionRate}}%</div><div class="l">conversion</div></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{subscribed}}</div><div class="l">reachable by push</div></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{waitingToSend}}</div><div class="l">queued to send</div></div>
</div>

<h2>Getting somebody here</h2>
<ol class="rules">
  <li>
    <h3>An invitation is one address in one box</h3>
    <p>There is one message for the whole community, written once. Nobody composes a sentence
      under pressure, and every invitation reads the same.
      <a href="{{invitesUrl}}">Invites</a>.</p>
    {{^invitesOn}}<p class="problem">Invitations are switched off for this community.</p>{{/invitesOn}}
  </li>
  <li>
    <h3>Three messages, and the third says it is the last</h3>
    {{#remindersOn}}<p>A welcome goes out. If nothing happens, a friendly reminder follows after
      <strong>{{reminderAfter}} day(s)</strong>, and a last note <strong>{{apologyAfter}} day(s)</strong>
      after that.</p>{{/remindersOn}}
    {{^remindersOn}}<p>Reminders are off, so only the welcome is sent.</p>{{/remindersOn}}
    <p class="hint">A fourth would be nagging, and a sequence with no visible end is one people
      report as spam &mdash; which costs the whole sending domain rather than the one message. The
      sequence stops the moment somebody joins or the invitation is revoked.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h3>One invitation, however many messages it took</h3>
    <p class="hint">One row, one token, one link. Somebody who joins after the second message is one
      conversion, not two &mdash; which is the same rule that stops resending inflating the rate.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h3>Members invite people, within a limit</h3>
    {{#membersMayInvite}}<p>Anybody here can invite up to <strong>{{memberDailyLimit}}</strong>
      people a day.</p>{{/membersMayInvite}}
    {{^membersMayInvite}}<p>Only people with the permission can invite.</p>{{/membersMayInvite}}
    <p class="hint">The people who bring somebody are the members, not the administrator. The daily
      limit is so one enthusiastic person cannot burn the sending domain for everybody.</p>
  </li>
</ol>

<h2>Getting somebody back</h2>
<ol class="rules">
  <li>
    <h3>The bell first, the email later</h3>
    {{#chatOn}}<p>Something said in a room you are not looking at lights the bell on every page. If
      nobody clears it within <strong>{{fuseMinutes}} minutes</strong>, it becomes a notification
      and goes out by email or push. Opening the room stops it.</p>{{/chatOn}}
    {{^chatOn}}<p class="hint">Chat is switched off for this community, so nothing uses this
      path.</p>{{/chatOn}}
    <p class="hint">The fuse is two database queries rather than a timer anybody holds, so it
      survives a restart and cannot fire twice. A message is meant to be seen in the room within
      seconds &mdash; mailing somebody straight away would be mailing them about a conversation
      they are already in.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h3>You are only told about a room you have been in</h3>
    <p class="hint">Somebody who has never opened a channel has not asked to hear about it. Mailing
      five hundred people because one of them said "morning" is how a community teaches everybody
      to filter it. One unread note per person per room, until they look.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h3>Two notification settings, because there are two events</h3>
    <p>A reply <em>to you</em> is a conversation waiting on an answer, and arrives straight away.
      Activity in a thread you are watching is news, and arrives as a daily summary.</p>
    <p class="hint">Everybody changes both on their own page. One setting would mean either mailing
      somebody about every comment in a busy thread or making them wait a day to learn a question
      was addressed to them.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h3>Joining a conversation is what makes you a watcher</h3>
    {{#boardOn}}<p class="hint">There is no subscribe button. A board where you have to remember to
      press one is a board where people miss the reply to their own comment &mdash; and it never
      tells you about your own.</p>{{/boardOn}}
    {{^boardOn}}<p class="hint">The board is switched off for this community.</p>{{/boardOn}}
  </li>
  <li>
    <h3>A push says who and where, never what</h3>
    {{#appOn}}<p class="hint">It crosses somebody else's infrastructure and lands on a lock screen.
      Its job is to bring a person back, not to tell them the thing &mdash; which matters most in
      exactly the community where it matters most. Signing out deletes the key it
      travels on.</p>{{/appOn}}
    {{^appOn}}<p class="hint">The installable app is switched off, so nothing here can push.</p>{{/appOn}}
  </li>
</ol>

<h2>Turning up in person</h2>
<ol class="rules">
  <li>
    <h3>Anybody can suggest something</h3>
    {{#calendarOn}}
      {{#suggestionsOn}}<p>Members put events forward and somebody with the permission decides.
        <a href="{{calendarUrl}}">Events</a>.</p>{{/suggestionsOn}}
      {{^suggestionsOn}}<p>Suggestions are off, so only people with the permission create
        events.</p>{{/suggestionsOn}}
    {{/calendarOn}}
    {{^calendarOn}}<p class="hint">The calendar is switched off for this community.</p>{{/calendarOn}}
    <p class="hint">A calendar only an administrator can write to is a programme published at a
      group. A calendar anybody can suggest to is a group deciding what it does &mdash; and the
      queue is what makes opening that door cost a screen to look at rather than the front page.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h3>More often beats more impressive</h3>
    <p class="hint">Friendship is made of hours, and a monthly dinner is thirty-six of them a year.
      Nothing on this server is worth building unless it makes the next one more likely.</p>
  </li>
</ol>

<h2>What this will not do</h2>
<ul class="cando">
  <li><span class="dim">never</span> rank anything by engagement</li>
  <li><span class="dim">never</span> send a fourth message in a sequence</li>
  <li><span class="dim">never</span> email somebody about a room they have never opened</li>
  <li><span class="dim">never</span> put the contents of a message in a push notification</li>
  <li><span class="dim">never</span> count an email open as evidence a person read it</li>
</ul>
<h2>How long a notification takes to work</h2>
<p class="hint">The gap between a phone buzzing and somebody arriving. Buckets rather than an
  average, because the average of "three people in a minute and one person tomorrow" is six hours,
  which describes nobody. Mostly-fast with a tail is a channel that works; mostly-tail is a channel
  people have turned off in their phone settings and nobody has noticed.</p>
<div class="scroller"><table class="bars">
  <tbody>{{#pushDelays}}{{^total}}<tr>
    <td class="dim">{{label}}</td>
    <td><span class="bar{{#unanswered}} dim{{/unanswered}}" style="width:{{width}}%">&nbsp;</span></td>
    <td class="num">{{count}}</td>
  </tr>{{/total}}{{/pushDelays}}</tbody>
</table></div>
<p class="hint">{{#pushDelays}}{{#total}}{{count}} answered.{{/total}}{{/pushDelays}}
  Only a tap counts &mdash; opening the app from a notification is the one honest signal this
  server gets that a push worked. Stamps are held in memory and written down every few minutes, so
  these numbers can be a little behind{{#pushWaiting}}; {{.}} waiting right now{{/pushWaiting}}.</p>
{{/content}}{{/shell}}
